  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-10",
    "title": "Saturn Behind the Moon",
    "explanation": " Explanation:  Sometimes Saturn disappears. It doesn't really go away, of course -- it just  disappears  from our view when  Earth's Moon  moves in front. Such a  Saturn ian eclipse, more formally called an  occultation , was visible in early 2025 along a small  swath  of  Earth  -- from  Greenland  through central  Europe  and into northern  Africa . The  featured image  is a digital fusion of three images, the first of just  Saturn  before it disappeared, and then two more of the Moon with  different exposure  lengths. The images were taken from  Sardinia ,  Italy , after sunset. The bright dot near Saturn is not a  moon of Saturn , but rather the star  85 Aquarii , which is further behind Saturn than Saturn is behind the Moon. The next time the  Moon  moves in front of Saturn, as visible from  some place on Earth , will be in 2031.    Tomorrow's picture:  small but mighty",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Moonsaturn6_Ortu_2124.jpg?w=2124&h=1582&fit=clip&crop=faces,focalpoint",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-10-saturn-behind-the-moon/",
    "credit": " Roberto Ortu ",
    "alt": "The curving surface of the Moon occupies the lower half of the image. Just above center the planet Saturn appears, far in the distance.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-11-10T09:13:21.438798+00:00"
